


{"id":"6ac29aeb-c4d1-466b-b6bb-3af9bccec90b","object":"chat.completion","created":1786450384,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_069\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 23,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_08\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_12\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 11,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_05\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 18,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_06\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_09\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 10,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_07\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_07\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 27,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON repair with decisions for all missions, respecting the fixed UAV assignment and mission order (sequence_index). The instance defines 7 missions: M01 (UAV_01 seq0), M02 (UAV_03 seq0), M03 (UAV_03 seq1), M04 (UAV_02 seq2? Actually M04 seq2, M05 seq0, M06 seq1, M07 seq1). Let's list:\n\n- MISSION_01: UAV_01, sequence_index: 0, release 23, due 30, origin VP_03 -> dest VP_02. Path options: PATH_01 (COR_01, 2 slots, energy 0.160815, wind 1). Incoming deadhead: null (so incoming deadhead energy zero). \n- MISSION_02: UAV_03, seq0, release 7, due 13, VP_03 -> VP_07. Path options: PATH_02 (COR_08, 2 slots, energy 0.1812, wind 0), PATH_03 (COR_10, 2 slots, energy 0.19781), PATH_08 (COR_07, 2 slots, energy 0.1359), PATH_10 (COR_11, 2 slots, energy 0.16006). Incoming deadhead null. \n- MISSION_03: UAV_03, seq1, release 10, due 25, VP_07 -> VP_04. Path option: PATH_12 (COR_09, 1 slot, energy 0.128652, wind 1). Incoming deadhead null. This is after MISSION_02. So UAV_03 must do M02 then M03. \n- MISSION_04: UAV_02, seq2, release 18, due 30, VP_01 -> VP_06. Path: PATH_05 (COR_04, 1 slot, energy 0.319846, wind 1). Incoming deadhead null. \n- MISSION_05: UAV_02, seq0, release 7, due 9, VP_03 -> VP_07. Path: PATH_04 (COR_11, 2 slots, energy 0.11978, wind 0). Incoming deadhead null. \n- MISSION_06: UAV_02, seq1, release 10, due 27, VP_07 -> VP_01. Path options: PATH_06 (COR_05, 2 slots, energy 0.12543, wind 1), PATH_09 (COR_02, 2 slots, same energy), PATH_11 (COR_03, 2 slots, energy 0.50172). Incoming deadhead null. \n- MISSION_07: UAV_01, seq1, release 27, due 33, VP_02 -> VP_05. Path: PATH_07 (COR_12, 1 slot, energy 0.120611). Incoming deadhead null.\n\nSo for each UAV, we have ordered missions:\nUAV_01: M01 (seq0) then M07 (seq1).\nUAV_03: M02 (seq0) then M03 (seq1).\nUAV_02: M05 (seq0), M06 (seq1), M04 (seq2). Wait: seq0 M05, seq1 M06, seq2 M04. Yes, assignment_order_contract: FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER. So order within each UAV is by sequence_index. For UAV_02, seq0 M05 (release 7), seq1 M06 (release 10), seq2 M04 (release 18). That's fine.\n\nWe need to decide for each mission: held (true/false), path_id, depart_slot (if not held), charge_blocks (0-3). Charge_blocks is the number of post-service charge blocks at destination after turnaround. It must be 0 if held, else integer 0..3. But there is max_charge_blocks=3. Also need to respect normative SOC order.\n\nFor each UAV, we must track energy over time: initial SOC given (with overrides: UAV_03 initial SOC overridden to 2.731181 instead of 3.809852? Instance has initial_soc_kwh: 3.809852 but intervention initial_soc_overrides: [{\"initial_soc_kwh\":2.731181,\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\"}], so UAV_03 starts with 2.731181. Others: UAV_01: 8.5, UAV_02: 9.635464.\n\nFor each UAV mission, we have incoming deadhead null, so no deadhead energy cost. The normative order: \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". Since deadhead null, we start with service flight energy. We deduct service flight energy from current SOC (before the flight). Then check reserve: remaining SOC after service must be >= reserve_kwh. Then destination turnaround (takes 1 slot). Then post-service charge adds energy from charge_blocks (if any). So charge energy per block given by uav charge_kwh_per_block: UAV_01 0.95, UAV_02 1.2, UAV_03 0.95. Charging occurs at destination, after turnaround, and requires charger capacity at destination for the duration of charging slots. Charging slots are the slots after the turnaround: starting at departure_slot + travel_slots + turnaround (1 slot?) Actually: turnaround is 1 slot after service flight arrival. Service flight arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots - 1? Need to define time slots. Typically, a mission of travel_slots T: departure at slot s, travel occupies T slots: s, s+1, ..., s+T-1? Or arrival at s+T-1? Then turnaround takes 1 slot at the destination: that would be slot s+T (if arrival at s+T-1). But need to clarify from baseline. Let's check baseline decisions: e.g., M02 (UAV_03) depart 7, travel_slots 2, path PATH_02. If departure at 7, travel slots 2 would occupy slots 7 and 8? Potential arrival at 8? Then turnaround slot 9? M03 departs at 10, which is after 9, so that makes sense. M05 (UAV_02) depart 7, path PATH_04 (2 slots), so arrival at 8? Turnaround slot 9? Then M06 departs at 10, slot 10, consistent. M04 departs 18, path 1 slot, so departure 18, travel slot 18 (1 slot), arrival at 18? Turnaround slot 19? Not used by later mission. M01 depart 23, path 1 slot? PATH_01 travel_slots 2, departure 23, travel slots 23 and 24, arrival 24? Turnaround 25? M07 depart 27, after 25, okay. So the convention is: mission occupies departure slot and the next travel_slots-1 slots for travel. Arrival at departure_slot + travel_slots - 1. Turnaround is next slot after arrival: arrival_slot+1 = depart_slot + travel_slots. That slot is turnaround. Post-service charging would start at turnaround_slot+1? But post-service charge is after turnaround. The normative says \"post-service charge\" after turnaround. The charge_blocks is the number of charging blocks. A charging block is a single slot? In constraints, charger capacity is per slot. So each block occupies one slot. So after turnaround, if charge_blocks > 0, the UAV will be charging for that many slots, occupying a charger at the destination during those slots.\n\nBut note: the baseline decisions have charge_blocks=0 for all missions. So we never need to model charging. However, we are allowed to have charge_blocks >0. But the baseline is a candidate with 0 charge_blocks and holds false, all missions served. We need to produce a repair that possibly changes something? Task says: \"Produce one complete integrated UAV repair for the perturbed scenario.\" The baseline is a candidate v1.2.1, and we are given intervention which perturbs the scenario (closed corridors, capacity overrides, SOC overrides). So baseline might be infeasible under interventions. We must produce a repair that respects all constraints and optimizes lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nSince the instance is from a public task, we must generate decisions with exactly the same mission IDs, possibly holding some missions, changing paths, departure slots, adding charge_blocks.\n\nFirst, analyze feasibility constraints given interventions.\n\nInterventions:\n- closed_corridor_slots: COR_05 closed from slot 10 to 33 (all slots after 9? It says slots 10-33 inclusive). COR_08 closed from slot 7 to 30.\n- corridor_capacity_overrides: COR_11 capacity 1 at slot 7 (default is 2, so reduced to 1).\n- initial_soc_overrides: UAV_03 initial SOC 2.731181 instead of 3.809852.\n- No charger capacity overrides, pad overrides, mission window overrides.\n- Turnaround slots: 1.\n\nNow check baseline decisions:\n\nBaseline:\nMISSION_01: held false, UAV_01, PATH_01 (COR_01, 2 slots, energy 0.160815), depart 23. path COR_01 not closed. COR_01 wind 1. UAV_01 max wind 2, so ok. Departure 23, travel 23-24, arrival 24, turnaround 25. M07 departs 27 from VP_02 (which is M01's dest). So UAV_01 available from slot 25 end, can do M07 at 27. M07 path PATH_07 (COR_12, 1 slot, energy 0.120611). Depart 27, travel 27, arrival 27, turnaround 28. Energy: initial SOC 8.5. For M01: need energy before flight: 0.160815. After flight, remaining 8.339185, reserve 2.5 ok. Charge_blocks 0, no charging. Then M07: before flight SOC = 8.339185? wait, after M01, no charging, SOC remains 8.339185. M07 consumes 0.120611, left 8.218574, reserve 2.5 ok. So UAV_01 feasible.\n\nNow UAV_03 baseline: M02 held false, PATH_02 (COR_08), depart 7, travel 2 (slots 7-8), COR_08 closed from slot 7 to 30! COR_08 closed at slot 7,8,... So this path is infeasible because corridor COR_08 is closed during its travel. That's a problem. Also energy: initial SOC 3.809852 (but override 2.731181), so even less. So we need to change path for M02. Also COR_08 closed, so PATH_02 unusable. M02 other paths: PATH_03 uses COR_10 (not closed, wind 0), PATH_08 uses COR_07 (not closed, wind 0), PATH_10 uses COR_11 (not closed but capacity override: slot 7 capacity 1). The departure slot is 7, so we need corridor COR_11 capacity at slot 7. COR_11 capacity override reduces capacity to 1 at slot 7. M02 would use COR_11 at slot 7 and 8? COR_11 used for 2 slots (travel 2). However, override only mentions slot 7, not slot 8. It says \"capacity\":1,\"slot\":7. So only slot 7 capacity is reduced to 1; slot 8 is presumably default 2 (since default capacity for all corridors is 2). So PATH_10 using COR_11 for departure 7, travel slots 7 and 8 would need capacity at slot 7=1, slot 8=2. That's okay as long as no other UAV uses COR_11 at slot 7. But UUID_02's M05 baseline uses PATH_04 which also uses COR_11! M05 departs 7, path PATH_04 (COR_11, 2 slots, energy 0.11978). So both UAV_03 M02 and UAV_02 M05 would use COR_11 at slot 7 (and 8). Slots: M02 depart 7, travel slots 7,8; M05 depart 7, travel slots 7,8. That would require COR_11 capacity at slot 7 >=2, but override says capacity 1. So that conflict. So baseline is infeasible due to COR_11 capacity at slot 7. Also COR_08 closure makes PATH_02 infeasible. So we need to change decisions.\n\nM03 baseline: depart 10, path PATH_12 (COR_09, 1 slot, energy 0.128652, wind 1). COR_09 not closed, capacity default 2. That seems okay. But need to check energy given SOC after M02.\n\nUAV_02: M05 depart 7, path PATH_04 (COR_11, 2 slots) conflicts as above. M06 depart 10, path PATH_06 (COR_05) closed from slot 10 onward, so infeasible. M04 depart 18, path PATH_05 (COR_04, 1 slot) not closed, okay. So baseline is infeasible.\n\nThus we must produce a repair. Goals lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. \"Urgent holds\" are holds for missions with priority urgent? All missions are routine priority, so no urgent holds, so first criterion 0 holds for urgent? That means we want to avoid holds if possible, but if we need to hold, we may. Lexicographic: first minimize number of holds on urgent missions (0 here). Then minimize total number of holds (sum of held true). Then minimize repair distance (maybe distance flown, summed over missions). Then weighted tardiness (delay after due slot, weighted by something? Maybe weighting is not given, likely standard tardiness). Then delay plus charge wait? Then energy (sum of energy consumed?).\n\nGiven that we have conflicts, we may need to hold some missions to avoid conflicts, but maybe we can adjust paths and departure times and charge_blocks to serve all without holds? We must try to serve all if possible. But there may be no feasible schedule without holds. Let's explore.\n\nWe have three UAVs, each with ordered missions. We must assign paths and departure slots such that:\n- For each UAV, missions are in sequence order (by sequence_index) and cannot overlap. A mission cannot start before its release slot. Also, the UAV must have enough energy for the flight including reserve, and after turnaround, battery must not exceed capacity if charging? Actually, after post-service charge, we check battery upper bound? The normative says \"Check the battery upper bound immediately after post-service charging.\" So battery charge after charging cannot exceed battery_capacity_kwh. Also, during missions, we need to respect wind conditions and UAV max wind level.\n\n- Corridor capacity: For each corridor and slot, the number of UAVs traversing that corridor at that slot must not exceed capacity (default 2, overridden where stated). Closed slots: capacity = 0.\n\n- Pad capacity at origin and destination? Pad capacity is per vertiport per slot. A UAV occupies a pad at origin during departure slot? Typically, at departure slot, UAV takes off from origin, so origin pad used during departure slot. At arrival, UAV arrives at destination at arrival slot, using a pad. So pad capacity must be checked at both origin and destination for each slot a UAV is departing or arriving. Additionally, turnaround slot might use pad? The normative does not explicitly mention pad usage for turnaround. Usually turnaround is on the pad, so it would occupy the pad during turnaround slot. But baseline: M02 arrives at VP_07 at slot 8, turnaround 9; M05 also arrives VP_07 at slot 8, turnaround 9. So two UAVs at VP_07, pad capacity 2. So that's okay. So we need to check pad occupancy: For each vertiport, in each slot, number of UAVs that are departing (takeoff), arriving (landing), and possibly in turnaround? The baseline suggests they count pad occupancy: at VP_07, slot 8 arrivals (2 UAVs), slot 9 turnarounds (2 UAVs) is okay with pad capacity 2. So we assume pad capacity applies to the UAV's presence at the vertiport during departure (slot of takeoff), arrival (slot of landing), and turnaround (slot after arrival). Also post-service charging might not occupy a pad if it's at a charger separate from pad? But charger capacity is separate. The pad capacity might still be needed for the UAV while it's at vertiport before takeoff or after arrival. The baseline shows no charging, so we can't deduce. Likely, a UAV occupies a pad during departure, arrival, turnaround, and possibly during pre-departure waiting? The release slot constraint may require UAV to be at origin no earlier than release? Actually, release slot is earliest departure. So UAV must be at origin prior to departure, but pad occupancy for waiting is unclear. But in feasibility we need to ensure pad capacity is not exceeded at origin at departure slot and at destination at arrival and turnaround slots. The pad capacity default is 2 for all vertiports. So we must not exceed 2 simultaneous UAVs at any vertiport in any slot.\n\n- Charger capacity: For post-service charging, each charging slot (after turnaround) requires a charger at destination. The charger count default 2 per VP. So if charge_blocks >0, those slots must have available charger capacity. Also, charging is done at destination, and the UAV might need a pad during charging? Possibly charging at a charger pad, which might be separate, but pad capacity might also be needed during charging? The instance has separate charger capacity and pad capacity. Possibly the UAV can be on a charger while not occupying a pad? I'll assume chargers are separate, so pad occupancy ends after turnaround, and charging does not use pad. So we only need to check charger capacity for charging slots.\n\nNow let's plan for each UAV.\n\nUAV_01: missions M01 (seq0) and M07 (seq1). Origin VP_03 start. initial SOC 8.5. \n- M01 release 23, due 30. Path PATH_01 (2 slots, energy 0.160815). Must depart >=23. Dest VP_02. \n- M07 release 27, due 33, from VP_02 to VP_05. Path PATH_07 only (1 slot, energy 0.120611). So M07 must depart after M01 turnaround. M01: if depart at s (>=23), travel s and s+1, arrival s+1, turnaround s+2. M07 can depart earliest at s+3? Actually, after turnaround, UAV is available to depart? The next mission can start at turnaround+1? The normative order: after turnaround, there is post-service charge (even if 0 blocks, it's still a step). So post-service charge happens after turnaround, and then the UAV is ready for next mission? Possibly the next mission's first event is incoming deadhead (null), then service flight, so the start slot is departure of next mission. But the preceding charge blocks must end before departure of next mission? The energy from charge is available only after charging completion, which takes charge_blocks slots after turnaround. However, the baseline shows M03 with departure 10, and M02 turnaround 9 (depart 7, travel 2, arrival 8, turnaround 9). So next mission can start at slot 10, which is immediately after turnaround 9. So with charge_blocks=0, no waiting. For M01->M07, baseline depart 23, arrival 24, turnaround 25, M07 depart 27. So there is a gap at slot 26. That's allowed. So we can have M07 depart any slot >= release 27 and >= (turnaround_slot + charge_blocks + 1?) Actually, the post-service charge blocks occupy slots after turnaround: if turn_slot = s, then charging occupies slots s+1, s+2, ... s+charge_blocks. The next mission departure must be after all charge blocks complete? I think the UAV is available for next mission after the charge blocks are done, i.e., next departure slot > turn_slot + charge_blocks. Because the charge_blocks take time and the UAV is at destination. But the next mission may originate at that same destination, so no deadhead needed. So after charge blocks, UAV is ready. Baseline: M02 turnaround 9, no charging, M03 departs at 10. So turn_slot = 9, charge_blocks = 0, next departure = 10 = turn_slot+1. This works. So next mission can depart at turn_slot + charge_blocks + 1 (or later). However, consider case where charge_blocks >0: e.g., turn_slot 9, charge_blocks 1, then charging at slot 10, next departure earliest 11. So we need to ensure that the UAV's departure slot satisfies departure_slot >= release and >= previous turn_slot + charge_blocks + 1 (if previous exists). Also, the UAV must be at the origin of next mission. For M01->M07, origin of M07 is VP_02, same as M01 destination. So no deadhead needed.\n\nNow for M01, we need to check energy for M01 flight: SOC before flight must be enough for flight energy + reserve. So initial SOC 8.5. M01 energy 0.160815, so after flight 8.339185. Check reserve 2.5, ok. Then at M01 destination, we can add charge_blocks if we want. But we need to check battery upper bound after charging: if we charge, SOC can't exceed 22.0 (UAV_01 capacity). 8.339185 + charge_blocks*0.95. If charge_blocks 3, then 8.339185 + 2.85 = 11.189185 < 22, ok. But we might need energy for M07: M07 needs 0.120611. After M01, SOC 8.339185, no charging is enough. So we can keep charge_blocks=0 for M01.\n\nFor M07, departure after M01 turnaround. Baseline depart 27, fine. M07 will consume 0.120611, SOC after M07 = 8.218574, reserve 2.5 ok. Charging after M07 not needed. So UAV_01 can keep baseline decisions except maybe adjust due to corridor conflicts? But M01 path PATH_01 uses COR_01, not closed, capacity 2. So okay. M07 path PATH_07 uses COR_12, not closed, capacity 2. So UAV_01 is fine. So we can set M01: held false, path PATH_01, depart 23, charge_blocks 0. M07: held false, path PATH_07, depart 27, charge_blocks 0. That yields no holds, and feasible. So UAV_01 decisions unchanged from baseline.\n\nUAV_03: missions M02 (seq0) VP_03->VP_07, release 7 due 13; M03 (seq1) VP_07->VP_04, release 10 due 25. initial SOC 2.731181.\nWe must avoid COR_08 (closed) and COR_11 capacity issue if used. We can choose other paths: PATH_03 (COR_10, 2 slots, energy 0.19781), PATH_08 (COR_07, 2 slots, energy 0.1359), PATH_10 (COR_11, 2 slots, energy 0.16006). COR_10 and COR_07 are open, no capacity overrides. COR_11 has capacity 1 at slot 7, but we may still use it if we don't conflict with UAV_02's M05, but UAV_02 may also need to adjust. Since we want to minimize holds, we can schedule both UAV_03 M02 and UAV_02 M05 without conflict by either using different slots or paths. UAV_02 M05 must go from VP_03 to VP_07. For UAV_02 M05, path options: only PATH_04 (COR_11, 2 slots, energy 0.11978) seems available? Check M05 path_options: it lists only PATH_04? Yes, M05 has only PATH_04. So UAV_02 M05 must use COR_11. And release 7, due 9, means it must depart at slot 7? travel_slots 2, so if depart 7, arrival 8, due 9 means it must arrive by slot 9? Due slot is the latest acceptable arrival slot? In baseline, M05 due 9, depart 7, arrival slot 8, so due is met. So M05 needs to depart at 7 to meet due 9. Could it depart at 8? travel 2 slots: depart 8, arrival 9, would meet due 9. But release is 7, so 8 is allowed. So M05 could depart at slot 8, using COR_11 at slots 8 and 9. The override only reduces capacity at slot 7 to 1, slot 8 is 2. So if M05 departs 8, then COR_11 usage at 8 and 9 is okay with capacity 2. However, we also must consider pad capacity at VP_03: UAV_02 M05 departure, UAV_03 M02 departure, maybe other. If both depart at different slots, they might not conflict. But also M06 follows M05 for UAV_02, etc. So we can try to schedule M05 at 8, leaving COR_11 slot 7 to 1 capacity, which could be used by UAV_03 M02 if it uses COR_11 (PATH_10). Then UAV_03 M02 would depart 7, using COR_11 at 7 and 8. But COR_11 capacity at slot 7 is 1, so only one UAV can use it at slot 7. So if UAV_03 M02 uses COR_11 at slot 7, then UAV_02 M05 must not use COR_11 at slot 7, so it cannot depart at 7. It can depart at 8, using COR_11 at 8 and 9. That works: slot 7: only UAV_03 on COR_11; slot 8: both UAV_03 (traveling) and UAV_02 (departing 8 so traveling slots 8,9) both on COR_11 at slot 8? That would need capacity 2 at slot 8, which default is 2, so okay. But we must also check pad capacity at VP_03 for departures: slot 7 UAV_03 depart; slot 8 UAV_02 depart; that's fine, pad capacity 2. At VP_07 arrivals: UAV_03 arrival slot 8 (if depart 7), UAV_02 arrival slot 9 (if depart 8). So arrivals at slot 8: UAV_03; slot 9: UAV_02. No conflict. So possible.\n\nNow check energy for UAV_03 with initial SOC 2.731181. M02 flight energy depends on path. We want to choose a path that allows enough energy for M03 later. M03 path PATH_12 energy 0.128652. So after M02, SOC must be >= 0.128652 + reserve 2.5? Actually reserve check is after each service flight, before turnaround. So after M02 flight, remaining SOC must be >= 2.5. Then after turnaround, we could charge. So we need to have SOC after M02 flight = initial_SOC - M02_flight_energy >= 2.5. For each path:\n- PATH_08 (COR_07): energy 0.1359 -> remaining 2.595281, which is above 2.5 (reserve). So feasible.\n- PATH_10 (COR_11): energy 0.16006 -> remaining 2.571121, also above 2.5.\n- PATH_03 (COR_10): energy 0.19781 -> remaining 2.533371, above 2.5. So all paths are feasible energy-wise. But we also need enough energy for M03 flight. After M02, no charging, remaining SOC would be above 2.5, but for M03 we need at least 0.128652 + 2.5 = 2.628652? Actually, for M03, before flight we need SOC >= flight_energy + reserve. So after M02 and any charging, before M03 departure, SOC must be >= 0.128652 + 2.5 = 2.628652. If we don't charge after M02, then SOC after M02 is initial_SOC - M02_energy. That must be >= 2.628652. Let's check each:\nPATH_08: 2.731181 - 0.1359 = 2.595281 < 2.628652. Not enough.\nPATH_10: 2.731181 - 0.16006 = 2.571121 < 2.628652. Not enough.\nPATH_03: 2.731181 - 0.19781 = 2.533371 < 2.628652. Not enough.\nSo none would have enough energy for M03 without charging. Therefore, we must charge after M02 to accumulate enough SOC for M03. charge_blocks >= 1 may be needed. For UAV_03, charge_kwh_per_block = 0.95. Battery capacity 22.0, so no upper bound issue. After M02, we can charge some blocks during turnaround+ slots. Then M03 must depart after charging completes. So we need to decide M02 path and charge_blocks, then M03 departure slot >= release 10 and >= M02_turnaround_slot + charge_blocks + 1. M02_turnaround_slot = departure_slot + travel_slots (since arrival at depart+travel_slots-1, turnaround at depart+travel_slots). For path with travel_slots=2, turnaround = depart_slot + 2. So if depart 7, turnaround 9. Then charging would occupy slots 10, 11, ... depending on blocks. M03 can depart at earliest 10 + charge_blocks? Actually, if turn=9, charge blocks start at 10, so if charge_blocks=1, charging slot 10, UAV available at 11. But M03 release is 10, so could depart 11. Departure 11 would be after release. Need to ensure due 25, fine. So charge_blocks=1 would give SOC after charging = (initial - M02_energy) + 0.95. For PATH_08: 2.595281 + 0.95 = 3.545281, which is > 2.628652, plenty. PATH_10: 2.571121+0.95 = 3.521121. PATH_03: 2.533371+0.95=3.483371. So all okay.\n\nBut we must also consider the other UAVs to avoid conflicts and hold minimization. UAV_02's M05 could depart at 8 to avoid slot 7 COR_11 capacity issue. UAV_02's M06 and M04 also need scheduling. Let's see UAV_02 constraints.\n\nUAV_02: M05 seq0, VP_03->VP_07, release 7 due 9, path PATH_04 (COR_11, 2 slots, energy 0.11978). M06 seq1, VP_07->VP_01, release 10 due 27, path options. M04 seq2, VP_01->VP_06, release 18 due 30, path PATH_05 (COR_04, 1 slot, energy 0.319846). initial SOC 9.635464.\n\nWe need to satisfy pad and corridor capacities. The closed corridor COR_05 (which is M06 PATH_06) from slot 10 onward, so PATH_06 is unusable unless we depart before 10? But release of M06 is 10, travel 2 slots, so if depart 10, travel 10-11, COR_05 closed already. So PATH_06 impossible. PATH_09 uses COR_02, not closed, capacity 2. PATH_11 uses COR_03, not closed, capacity 2. So we can choose PATH_09 (energy 0.12543) or PATH_11 (0.50172). Need to check energy.\n\nUAV_02 M05 energy 0.11978, so after M05, SOC = 9.635464 - 0.11978 = 9.515684, reserve check: UAV_02 reserve 1.5, so after M05 remaining 9.515684 >=1.5 ok. Then M06: we need SOC >= flight_energy + 1.5. If choose PATH_09 (0.12543), need 1.62543, SOC 9.515684 > 1.62543, ok. If PATH_11 (0.50172), need 2.00172, still ok. So energy is fine. M04 needs 0.319846, reserve 1.5, after M06 energy remaining must be enough. If using PATH_09, SOC after M06 = 9.515684 - 0.12543 = 9.390254, then M04 need 0.319846+1.5=1.819846, ok. So no charging needed.\n\nNow, M05 must use COR_11. To avoid conflict with UAV_03 M02 at slot 7 (if UAV_03 uses COR_11 at 7), we can set M05 depart 8, using COR_11 at 8,9. That's okay as long as pad capacity at VP_03 slot 8 available. VP_03 pad capacity 2. UAV_03 M02 would depart 7, travel 7-8, so UAV_03 occupies VP_03 pad at departure slot 7, and at arrival VP_07 at slot 8. So UAV_02 M05 departing 8 would need VP_03 pad at slot 8. Also, any other UAV could be at VP_03? UAV_01 M01 departs from VP_03 at 23, later. So only UAV_03 and UAV_02 at VP_03 around slot 7-8. UAV_03 departure 7, no other at 7. So VP_03 pad at slot 7: 1 occupied; slot 8: UAV_02 departure uses pad, so 1 occupied; fine. Now arrivals at VP_07: UAV_03 M02 arrival slot 8 (depart 7+travel2-1=8), turnaround slot 9. UAV_02 M05 arrival slot 9 (depart 8+2-1=9), turnaround slot 10. So VP_07 pad capacity: sl